/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An equation of state is any relation among pressure, volume and temperature for a fixed mass.
   - p is absolute pressure in Pa; v is molar volume V/n in m³/mol; Ru is the universal gas constant, 8.314 J/(mol·K); T is absolute temperature in K, never °C.
   - Engineers usually work per kilogram, not per mole.
   - V is total volume in m³; m is mass in kg; R is the specific gas constant in J/(kg·K); Ru = 8.314 J/(mol·K) is the universal gas constant; M is molar mass in kg/mol.
   - The same law defines a temperature scale.
   - Two facts spoil the model.
   - Deviations grow as the gas is compressed towards saturation, or pushed to high pressure at a temperature well above the critical temperature.
   - A real gas, also called a non-ideal gas in some syllabi, therefore needs a better equation of state.
   - Van der Waals repaired the ideal law with two constants.
   - p is pressure in Pa; v is molar volume in m³/mol; T is in K; Ru = 8.314 J/(mol·K); a is the attraction constant in Pa·m⁶/mol²; b is the excluded volume in m³/mol, roughly four times the molecular volume.
*/
