/* book Worked example 2: Q: Squeeze the same mole of carbon dioxide into 0.3 litre (v = 0.0003 m³/mol) and hold it at 350 K. Find the pressure from the ideal gas law and from the van der Waals equation, with the same a and b. Reference property data give 7.37 MPa for this state. Which equation is closer?
   1. Ru T = 8.314 × 350 = 2909.9 J/mol.
   2. Ideal gas law: p = Ru T/v = 2909.9/0.0003 = 9 699 667 Pa = 9.700 MPa.
   3. Free volume: v − b = 0.0003 − 0.00004267 = 0.00025733 m³/mol. Repulsive part: 2909.9/0.00025733 = 11 308 048 Pa = 11.308 MPa.
   4. Attraction part: a/v² = 0.3640/(0.0003)² = 0.3640/9 × 10⁻⁸ = 4 044 444 Pa = 4.044 MPa.
   5. Van der Waals pressure: p = 11.308 − 4.044 = 7.264 MPa.
   6. Compare with 7.37 MPa: the ideal law is 2.33 MPa (32 per cent) too high; van der Waals is 0.11 MPa (1.5 per cent) low. At this density attraction matters, and van der Waals captures most of it.
   Answer: Ideal p = 9.70 MPa, van der Waals p = 7.26 MPa; van der Waals is far closer to the true 7.37 MPa. */
