/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A real gas approaches ideal behaviour only at low pressure and high temperature.
   - p is absolute pressure in Pa or kPa; v is specific volume in m³/kg; R is the gas constant in J/(kg·K) or kJ/(kg·K); T is absolute temperature in kelvin; videal = RT/p.
   - To compare gases, scale by critical properties: pR = p/pc, TR = T/Tc, and vR = v/vc.
   - Written with vR = v/vc, the van der Waals equation of 5.7 loses its constants: (pR + 3/vR²)(3vR − 1) = 8TR.
   - The chart plots pR on the horizontal axis and Z on the vertical axis.
   - Do not use gauge pressure or Celsius in pR and TR.
   - Once Z is known, v = ZRT/p.
   - v is specific volume in m³/kg; Z is the compressibility factor from the chart; R is the gas constant in kJ/(kg·K); T is kelvin; p is absolute pressure in kPa.
   - The chart is a hand tool for quick estimates.
*/
