/* book Worked example: Q: A CNG cylinder of internal volume 60 litres is filled with methane to 20 MPa at 300 K. For methane Tc = 190.6 K, pc = 4.60 MPa and R = 0.5183 kJ/(kg·K). Estimate the mass of gas (a) with the ideal gas law, (b) with Z from the generalized compressibility chart.
   1. Reduced temperature: TR = 300/190.6 = 1.574. Reduced pressure: pR = 20/4.60 = 4.35.
   2. Ideal gas: v = RT/p = 0.5183 × 300/20 000 = 155.49/20 000 = 0.007775 m³/kg, so m = V/v = 0.060/0.007775 = 7.72 kg.
   3. Chart: at TR ≈ 1.57 and pR ≈ 4.35 the chart gives Z ≈ 0.83 (Figure 5.8.1).
   4. Real gas: v = ZRT/p = 0.83 × 0.007775 = 0.006453 m³/kg, so m = 0.060/0.006453 = 9.30 kg.
   5. The ideal law underestimates the fill by 9.30 − 7.72 = 1.58 kg, about 17 per cent. Reference data for methane give Z = 0.828 and 9.32 kg, so the chart is within 0.3 per cent.
   Answer: TR = 1.57, pR = 4.35, Z ≈ 0.83; the cylinder holds about 9.3 kg of methane, not the 7.7 kg the ideal law predicts. */
